<header class="hero"><div class="shell"><a href="/guides/">← All guides</a><p class="eyebrow">Nashville market guide</p><h1>{title}</h1><p class="dek">New-home sales jumped nationally in October. The more interesting question is whether builders are converting buyers by changing the deal.</p><p class="byline">By Jason Kaczmarski · November 27, 2026 · 5 minute read</p></div></header>
<div class="shell body">

<p class="lead">The housing market is still difficult. Mortgage rates are above 7%, buyer traffic is weak, and Nashville resale sellers are competing for a limited pool of motivated buyers. Yet one part of the market just produced a surprisingly strong number: new-home sales.</p>

<p>The U.S. Census Bureau reported that sales of new single-family homes rose to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of <strong>701,000 in October</strong>, up <strong>14.2% from September</strong> and <strong>7.5% from October 2025</strong>. The median new-home sale price was <strong>$391,300</strong>, down 3.2% year over year. <a href="https://www.census.gov/construction/nrs/current/index.html">Source: U.S. Census Bureau</a>.</p>

<p>One month does not establish a lasting trend, and the Census estimates carry wide margins of error. But the direction is interesting because it comes at a time when builders themselves remain deeply pessimistic about buyer traffic.</p>

<h2>Builders do not suddenly think the market is good</h2>

<p>The National Association of Home Builders reported a November Housing Market Index reading of <strong>25</strong>. Its prospective-buyer-traffic index fell to <strong>16</strong>, the weakest reading in 15 years outside the early-pandemic disruption.</p>

<p>At the same time, <strong>46% of builders reported cutting prices</strong> in November, the highest share in the post-Covid period, while <strong>68% were using sales incentives</strong>. <a href="https://www.nahb.org/news-and-economics/press-releases/2026/11/builder-sentiment-continues-to-fall-on-affordability-concerns">Source: NAHB</a>.</p>

<p>That combination is the part I think matters most.</p>

<section class="callout"><p class="eyebrow">The possibility worth watching</p><h2>Builders may not have more buyers. They may simply be doing more to convert the buyers who exist.</h2><p>Lower asking prices, rate buydowns, closing-cost help and other incentives can change a buyer's monthly payment and cash needed at closing in ways a traditional resale seller may struggle to match.</p></section>

<h2>The Nashville builder market is showing the same behavior</h2>

<p>Dwellmark, an independent tracker of published builder inventory, reported <strong>80 asking-price cuts totaling about $1.16 million</strong> across the Nashville metro during the seven days ending November 26. It was tracking 1,096 homes from 18 builders, with 305 active incentives and 547 tracked homes showing some form of published offer.</p>

<p>Its coverage is not the entire MLS and should not be treated as a marketwide statistic. But it provides a useful real-time look at how builders are adjusting inventory. <a href="https://dwellmark.house/deals?metro=nashville">Source: Dwellmark</a>.</p>

<p>Recent tracked cuts included a roughly $65,700 reduction in Mount Juliet, a $60,000 reduction in Spring Hill, and several other reductions exceeding $50,000 around Middle Tennessee. These are published asking-price changes observed by the tracker, not appraisals or proof of final negotiated sales prices.</p>

<h2>Mortgage demand also bounced</h2>

<p>The Mortgage Bankers Association reported that purchase mortgage applications increased <strong>10% in the week ending November 20</strong>, reaching their strongest weekly pace since September. That occurred even though MBA's measured 30-year mortgage rate rose slightly to 7.38%.</p>

<p>Purchase applications were still 16% below the same week last year on an unadjusted basis, so this is not evidence of a full demand recovery. But it does suggest that buyers have not disappeared completely. <a href="https://www.mba.org/news-and-research/newsroom/news/2026/11/25/mortgage-applications-increase-in-latest-mba-weekly-survey">Source: Mortgage Bankers Association</a>.</p>

<p>Freddie Mac's latest weekly average was <strong>7.21%</strong>, down from 7.28% the week before. <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms">Source: Freddie Mac</a>.</p>

<h2>Why this matters to a resale seller</h2>

<p>It would be easy to interpret weak resale activity as proof that there are simply no buyers. I think that is too simplistic.</p>

<p>The better question may be: <strong>What is it taking to get a buyer to act?</strong></p>

<p>A builder can reduce a completed home's price, offer a below-market mortgage rate through an affiliated lender, contribute toward closing costs, and package upgrades into the deal. A resale seller usually has fewer tools and less margin.</p>

<p>That means a resale home can be reasonably priced compared with historical resale comps and still struggle if the buyer's current alternative is a new home with a more attractive monthly payment or lower cash-to-close requirement.</p>

<h2>This does not prove builders are taking market share in Nashville</h2>

<p>I want to be careful here. The October new-home sales report is national, while Nashville's local resale and new-construction contract data are not yet current enough to prove that builders are gaining local market share right now.</p>

<p>That is the next number I want to see: a fresh Nashville-area contract comparison separating <strong>new construction from resale</strong>, especially in Wilson and Rutherford counties.</p>

<p>If new-construction contracts begin outperforming resale while builders continue discounting aggressively, that would be strong evidence that buyers are responding to the package builders are offering.</p>

<h2>What I would watch heading into December</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Do local builder price cuts continue?</strong> Year-end inventory pressure can produce unusually aggressive deals.</li>
<li><strong>Do purchase applications hold their recent gain?</strong> One strong week is encouraging; several would be more meaningful.</li>
<li><strong>Do Nashville new-build contracts outperform resale?</strong> This is the clearest test of the theory.</li>
<li><strong>Do builders reset base pricing?</strong> Clearing a few completed homes is different from lowering the pricing structure for future inventory.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The takeaway</h2>

<p>Builders are still telling us the market is difficult. Their traffic numbers are poor and their confidence is low.</p>

<p>But they are also responding more aggressively than many resale sellers can: cutting prices, subsidizing financing and offering incentives. October's jump in new-home sales suggests those tools may be helping convert buyers.</p>

<p>For Nashville resale sellers—especially in Mount Juliet, Lebanon, Smyrna, Murfreesboro, Antioch and other areas with substantial new construction—that makes nearby builder inventory part of the competitive analysis, whether or not the builder home looks like a traditional comp.</p>

<p>If you're considering selling in Middle Tennessee, I can help compare your home not only with recent resale sales, but also with nearby builder inventory, current incentives and the buyer's likely monthly-payment alternatives.</p>

<p>Related: <a href="/guides/nashville-builder-price-cuts-november-2026/">Nashville Builders Are Cutting Prices. Resale Sellers Should Pay Attention.</a></p>

<p><small>Market conditions, builder pricing, incentives and mortgage rates can change quickly. National new-home sales estimates are subject to revision. Builder inventory examples are based on published asking prices and should be independently verified before making a purchase or sale decision.</small></p>

